__registerContent("newspaper/polity/2026-08-04/births-deaths-amendment", {"notes":{"news-2026-08-04-births-deaths":{"newsType":true,"date":"4 August 2026","paper":"PRS / Parliament","subject":"Polity / GS-2 (Governance, Government Policies)","summary":"Parliament passed the Registration of Births and Deaths (Amendment) Bill, 2026 (Lok Sabha on 31 July 2026, Rajya Sabha on 4 August 2026), amending the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969. The amendment makes delayed registration more stringent by graded scrutiny: a delay of more than 1 year but up to 2 years needs an order of the District Magistrate / Sub-Divisional Magistrate / authorised Executive Magistrate, while a delay of more than 2 years now needs an order of a Judicial Magistrate First Class (JMFC). The nodal ministry is the Ministry of Home Affairs; the key provision amended is Section 13(3). The aim is to encourage timely reporting and improve the integrity of civil-registration data.","keyPoints":[{"heading":"What the 2026 Amendment Changes (Section 13(3))","points":["Up to 1 year delay: ordinary/normal registration procedure","More than 1 year but up to 2 years: order of District Magistrate / Sub-Divisional Magistrate / Executive Magistrate authorised by the DM + verification + prescribed fee","More than 2 years: order of a JUDICIAL Magistrate First Class (JMFC) + verification + prescribed fee","Memory hook: '1 → 2 → Judicial' — longer delay = higher scrutiny (administrative scrutiny escalates to judicial scrutiny)","Earlier (1969 Act): any delay beyond 1 year needed a DM/SDM/Executive Magistrate order — there was no separate 'beyond 2 years → JMFC' category"]},{"heading":"Legislative Timeline & Basics","points":["Introduced in Lok Sabha: 29 July 2026","Passed by Lok Sabha: 31 July 2026; passed by Rajya Sabha: 4 August 2026","Parent legislation: Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969","Nodal Ministry: Ministry of Home Affairs","Do NOT confuse with the Registration of Births and Deaths (Amendment) Act, 2023 (in force 1 October 2023) — the 2026 law is a further, narrower amendment"]},{"heading":"Why the Amendment (Objectives)","points":["Encourage timely registration by escalating scrutiny with delay","Improve accuracy of civil-registration data and curb fraudulent/retrospective entries","Strengthen demographic databases (fertility, mortality, sex ratio, population change)","Support governance: welfare targeting, pensions, insurance, inheritance, health planning, electoral/administrative databases"]},{"heading":"Civil Registration System (CRS) vs Census","points":["CRS: continuous registration of individual vital events (births/deaths/stillbirths); functions under the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India (MHA)","Census: periodic enumeration giving a comprehensive population snapshot (demographic, social, economic characteristics)","CRS is NOT a substitute for the Census — the two complement each other","State Governments have rule-making powers under Section 30 of the 1969 Act"]},{"heading":"Concerns / Challenges (for Mains balance)","points":["Accessibility: stricter procedures may disproportionately affect people in remote rural, tribal and conflict/disaster-affected areas and disadvantaged communities — a genuine late registrant faces extra bureaucratic hurdles","Judicial burden: moving registrations delayed beyond two years to JMFCs could increase the workload of the lower judiciary","Digital divide: increasing digitisation of civil registration needs connectivity, digital literacy, interoperable databases and cybersecurity","Privacy: civil-registration data is highly sensitive; integration of databases raises concerns of data protection, unauthorised access, surveillance and data sharing","Federal concerns: greater central standardisation/integration must be balanced with States' constitutional and administrative role (Section 30 rule-making)"]},{"heading":"Constitutional / Legal Angle","points":["Article 21 (right to life & personal liberty): interpreted to include dignity and privacy — digital vital-statistics databases need privacy and data-protection safeguards","Article 14 (equality): the registration system should not create unreasonable barriers for disadvantaged groups","Governance principle: a good civil-registration system must balance accuracy + accessibility + privacy + accountability"]},{"heading":"Cross-links","points":["GS-2 Governance: government policy, data governance, Centre–State roles in registration","GS-1 Demography: vital statistics, fertility/mortality, sex ratio at birth","GS-3 Technology: digital/integrated civil registration, data infrastructure, cybersecurity","GS-2 Federalism: standardisation vs States' role (Section 30); cooperative federalism","Essay/Mains: 'Reliability of civil registration is fundamental to evidence-based governance'"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969","explanation":"The parent legislation providing the legal framework for compulsory registration of births and deaths in India. A birth/death certificate is legal proof of the event and admissible as evidence. Substantially amended in 2023 (in force 1 October 2023), and further amended in 2026 to tighten delayed registration."},{"term":"Section 13(3) (the provision amended)","explanation":"Deals with delayed registration. Post-2026 it creates two tiers: delay >1 year and ≤2 years requires a DM/SDM/authorised Executive Magistrate's order; delay >2 years requires a Judicial Magistrate First Class order. Both require verification of correctness and payment of the prescribed fee."},{"term":"Civil Registration System (CRS)","explanation":"The continuous, permanent, compulsory recording of vital events (births, deaths, stillbirths). Operates under the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India, Ministry of Home Affairs. Feeds demographic statistics, public-health planning and welfare delivery. Distinct from the periodic Census."},{"term":"Executive Magistrate vs Judicial Magistrate First Class","explanation":"An Executive Magistrate (referred to under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023) performs executive/administrative-magisterial functions; the DM/SDM tier handles 1–2 year delays. A Judicial Magistrate First Class (JMFC) performs judicial functions and, under the 2026 amendment, is required for delays beyond 2 years — reflecting escalation from administrative to judicial scrutiny."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["The 2026 amendment reflects a shift in India's civil-registration challenge: from achieving universal coverage (birth registration rose ~86.6% in 2014 to ~99.1% in 2024; death registration ~72.5% to ~99.4%) to ensuring the accuracy, timeliness and integrity of the data.","Graded scrutiny — DM/SDM for 1–2 year delays and JMFC for delays beyond 2 years — introduces proportionate safeguards: the longer the delay, the greater the risk of fraudulent or retrospective entries, so the higher the level of verification.","Reliable vital statistics underpin evidence-based governance: welfare targeting, removal of deceased beneficiaries from databases, pensions, insurance, inheritance, health and population policy, and electoral/administrative records.","Concerns must be balanced against benefits: stricter procedures may disadvantage people in remote, tribal or disaster-affected areas; shifting 2-year-plus cases to JMFCs may add to lower-judiciary workload; digitisation raises digital-divide, privacy and data-protection issues; and central standardisation must respect States' Section 30 rule-making role.","Way forward: simplify genuine delayed-registration procedures, strengthen local registration infrastructure and doorstep/mobile registration, provide multilingual digital platforms with interoperability and strong data-protection safeguards, offer legal aid for genuine delayed cases, and audit CRS databases periodically. The goal is universal, timely, accurate, accessible and privacy-protected registration."],"prelims":["Parent Act: Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969; nodal ministry: Ministry of Home Affairs","Provision amended: Section 13(3) (delayed registration)","Delay >1 year and ≤2 years → DM / SDM / authorised Executive Magistrate + verification + fee","Delay >2 years → Judicial Magistrate First Class (JMFC) + verification + fee","Lok Sabha passed 31 July 2026; Rajya Sabha passed 4 August 2026 (introduced 29 July 2026)","Previous amendment: 2023 (in force 1 October 2023) — do not confuse with 2026","CRS operates under the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India (MHA); States have rule-making powers under Section 30","Coverage: birth registration ~86.6% (2014) → ~99.1% (2024); death registration ~72.5% (2014) → ~99.4% (2024); 3.5 lakh+ registration centres"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-08-04-births-deaths":{"title":"Registration of Births and Deaths (Amendment) Act, 2026","questions":[{"q":"The Registration of Births and Deaths (Amendment) Act, 2026 amends which parent legislation?","options":["The Census Act, 1948","The Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969","The Aadhaar Act, 2016","The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 2026 amendment amends the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969."},{"q":"Under the 2026 amendment, registration of a birth or death reported after a delay of more than two years requires an order of:","options":["The District Magistrate","The Sub-Divisional Magistrate","An authorised Executive Magistrate","A Judicial Magistrate First Class"],"correct":3,"explain":"For delays exceeding two years, the amendment requires an order of a Judicial Magistrate First Class (JMFC) — escalating from administrative to judicial scrutiny."},{"q":"For a delay of more than one year but not exceeding two years, registration requires an order of:","options":["A Judicial Magistrate First Class","The District Magistrate / Sub-Divisional Magistrate / authorised Executive Magistrate","The Registrar General of India","The High Court"],"correct":1,"explain":"A delay of >1 year but ≤2 years requires an order of the DM/SDM/authorised Executive Magistrate, with verification and the prescribed fee."},{"q":["Consider the following statements regarding the Registration of Births and Deaths (Amendment) Act, 2026:","I. It amends the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969.","II. A delay of more than one year but up to two years requires the approval of a Judicial Magistrate First Class.","III. The Civil Registration System functions under the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India.","Which of the statements given above are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":1,"explain":"Statements I and III are correct. Statement II is wrong — a delay of >1 year but ≤2 years needs a DM/SDM/authorised Executive Magistrate order, not a JMFC (which is required only for delays beyond two years)."},{"q":"Which provision of the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969 was amended by the 2026 Act to alter the delayed-registration procedure?","options":["Section 13(3)","Section 30","Section 17A","Section 44(3)"],"correct":0,"explain":"Section 13(3), which governs delayed registration, was amended to introduce graded scrutiny based on the length of delay."},{"q":"Which Ministry is the nodal ministry for the Registration of Births and Deaths Act and the Civil Registration System?","options":["Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation","Ministry of Home Affairs","Ministry of Law and Justice"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Act and the Civil Registration System (under the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India) fall under the Ministry of Home Affairs."},{"q":["With reference to the Civil Registration System (CRS) and the Census, consider the following statements:","I. The CRS involves continuous recording of vital events, while the Census is a periodic enumeration.","II. The CRS is a substitute for the Census.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only statement I is correct. The CRS is NOT a substitute for the Census — the two complement each other, one giving continuous vital-event data and the other a periodic population snapshot."},{"q":"The 2026 amendment is best described as:","options":["A modernisation of civil registration through digital and integrated databases","A measure making delayed registration more stringent through graded scrutiny","The creation of a new national population register","A transfer of registration powers exclusively to the Centre"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 2026 amendment is narrow — it makes delayed registration more stringent ('1 → 2 → Judicial'). The broader modernisation/integration changes were largely made by the 2023 amendment."},{"q":["Arrange the following events in chronological order:","1. Bill introduced in Lok Sabha","2. Passed by Lok Sabha","3. Passed by Rajya Sabha","Select the correct order (earliest first):"],"options":["1 → 2 → 3","2 → 1 → 3","3 → 2 → 1","1 → 3 → 2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Introduced in Lok Sabha on 29 July 2026, passed by Lok Sabha on 31 July 2026, and passed by Rajya Sabha on 4 August 2026."},{"q":["Match the delay period with the required authority under the 2026 amendment:","I. >1 year and ≤2 years — a) Judicial Magistrate First Class","II. >2 years — b) District Magistrate / SDM / authorised Executive Magistrate","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-a","I-a, II-b","Both need JMFC","Both need only the DM"],"correct":0,"explain":"A delay of >1 year and ≤2 years needs a DM/SDM/authorised Executive Magistrate (b); a delay of >2 years needs a Judicial Magistrate First Class (a)."},{"q":"Under which section of the 1969 Act do State Governments have rule-making powers to carry out the purposes of the Act?","options":["Section 13(3)","Section 30","Section 17A","Section 8"],"correct":1,"explain":"Section 30 of the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969 gives State Governments rule-making powers — relevant to the federalism dimension."},{"q":"The Civil Registration System (CRS) in India functions under:","options":["The National Statistical Office","The Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India (Ministry of Home Affairs)","The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","NITI Aayog"],"correct":1,"explain":"The CRS operates under the Registrar General & Census Commissioner of India, which is under the Ministry of Home Affairs."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. Birth registration coverage rose from about 86.6% (2014) to 99.1% (2024).","II. Death registration coverage rose from about 72.5% (2014) to 99.4% (2024).","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both figures are correct per the reported government data, reflecting a shift from a coverage problem to a data-accuracy/timeliness challenge."},{"q":"The earlier (2023) amendment to the Registration of Births and Deaths Act came into force on:","options":["1 January 2023","1 October 2023","26 January 2024","1 July 2024"],"correct":1,"explain":"The 2023 amendment's provisions came into force on 1 October 2023 — distinct from the narrower 2026 amendment on delayed registration."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the 2026 amendment:","I. It amends Section 13(3) of the 1969 Act.","II. The nodal ministry is the Ministry of Home Affairs.","III. Both delay categories require verification of correctness and payment of the prescribed fee.","How many of the statements are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"All three are correct — the amendment changes Section 13(3), the MHA is the nodal ministry, and both delay tiers require verification and the prescribed fee."},{"q":"An Executive Magistrate (referred to in the delayed-registration procedure) is appointed under which law?","options":["The Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (BNSS)","The Representation of the People Act, 1951","The Registration Act, 1908","The Aadhaar Act, 2016"],"correct":0,"explain":"The Bill refers to an Executive Magistrate appointed under the BNSS, 2023 — performing executive/administrative-magisterial functions (as distinct from the judicial JMFC)."},{"q":"A key distinction the amendment relies upon is that a Judicial Magistrate First Class exercises _____ functions, whereas an Executive Magistrate exercises _____ functions.","options":["judicial; executive/administrative","executive; judicial","legislative; judicial","administrative; legislative"],"correct":0,"explain":"The JMFC exercises judicial functions while the Executive Magistrate exercises executive/administrative functions — hence escalation to a JMFC for delays beyond two years signals higher (judicial) scrutiny."},{"q":"Which of the following best captures the guiding principle of the 2026 amendment?","options":["Shorter delay = higher scrutiny","Longer delay = higher scrutiny","All registrations require judicial approval","Registration is optional after two years"],"correct":1,"explain":"The amendment escalates scrutiny with the length of delay — 'longer delay = higher scrutiny' (administrative for 1-2 years, judicial beyond 2 years)."},{"q":"Which of the following is cited as a concern/challenge of the 2026 amendment?","options":["It abolishes the Civil Registration System","Stricter procedures may disadvantage people in remote/tribal areas and add to lower-judiciary workload","It removes the requirement of a birth certificate entirely","It transfers registration to the private sector"],"correct":1,"explain":"Concerns include reduced accessibility for remote/tribal/disadvantaged groups and added workload on JMFCs, alongside digital-divide, privacy and federalism issues."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the CRS and the Census:","I. The CRS continuously records individual vital events.","II. The Census is a periodic population enumeration.","III. The CRS can fully replace the Census.","How many of the statements are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":1,"explain":"Statements I and II are correct; III is wrong — the CRS is not a substitute for the Census. The two are complementary."}]}}});
